TY  - JOUR
TI  - Beyond observed COP : incorporating operational context into control potential in heat pump system
AU  - Mun, J. Y.
AU  - Jo, H. G.
AU  - Kim, H. Y.
AU  - Jeong, Y. J.
AU  - Kim, S. H.
AU  - Park, C. S.
PY  - under review
AB  - The coefficient of performance (COP) alone cannot reliably evaluate heating, ventilation, and air-conditioning (HVAC) control performance because the observed outcomes reflect the combined effects of the implemented control system and operating context. To account for context dependence, this study proposes an interval-level diagnostic framework for estimating the control-related improvement potential. The framework jointly quantifies the control and corresponding outcome gaps relative to a data-supported reference representing favorable operation under comparable conditions. A probabilistic reference control is learned from unlabeled historical data by weighting the controls associated with superior context-relative outcomes. A conditional outcome model then predicts the performance under both observed and reference controls within the same context, enabling a model-based counterfactual comparison. Finally, the total outcome gap is decomposed into the predicted improvement from reference-control substitution and any remaining discrepancy. The framework was demonstrated using 5-min field data from a hospital variable refrigerant flow (VRF) system, evaluating target high- and low-pressure settings as the controls and COP as the outcome. Models trained on 2023 cooling-season data were evaluated on held-out 2024 data. The conditional outcome model achieved a mean absolute error of 0.15 COP. Advantage weighting shifted the learned reference toward controls, yielding favorable contextual outcomes and producing higher predicted COP values than uniform weighting in 92.16% of held-out contexts. Contextual diagnosis revealed that nearly identical COP values often corresponded to markedly different control gaps, confirming that observed outcomes alone do not always characterize control performance. Similar COP shortfalls varied substantially in their predicted improvement under reference-control substitution and in their remaining unassessed discrepancies. By linking control gaps to predicted outcomes under comparable conditions, this framework identifies precisely when control adjustments yield meaningful efficiency gains.
KW  - control diagnosis
KW  - contextual reference
KW  - counterfactual comparison
KW  - uncertainty quantification
KW  - variable refrigerant flow system
KW  - field data
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Effects of training data coverage on the replicative and predictive validity of OAC preheating coil models
AU  - Jeong, Y. J.
AU  - Jo, H. G.
AU  - Kim, J. H.
AU  - Kim, S. H.
AU  - Mun, J. H.
AU  - Park, C. S.
PY  - under review
AB  - The advantages of different HVAC modeling approaches should not be evaluated independently of the measured data available for model development. In particular, the extent to which training data cover the operating domain may determine whether the inherent characteristics of grey-box, hybrid, and black-box models can be effectively leveraged. This study investigates the influence of training data coverage on the replicative and predictive validity of these three modeling approaches. The models were developed to predict the outlet air temperature of a preheating coil in an outdoor air-conditioning system located in a real-world semiconductor manufacturing facility. The operating space was defined by the coil’s inlet air temperature, inlet water temperature, and valve opening ratio and subsequently discretized into a three-dimensional grid. Training datasets with progressively increasing coverage were developed, and model performance was evaluated using fixed test datasets representing observed and unobserved operating regions. An additional experiment was conducted with the total number of training samples held constant to isolate the influence of operating-space coverage from that of data quantity. The three approaches exhibited distinctly different responses to training data coverage. The grey-box model showed a progressive increase in error with broader coverage, with predictive MAE increasing from 2.78 to 3.13 °C, indicating limitations inherent in its fixed parameterization. The hybrid model was comparatively insensitive to coverage and maintained predictive MAEs of 1.45–1.91 °C, demonstrating the benefit of embedded physical relationships when the training data represented only a limited portion of the operating region. In contrast, the black-box model was highly dependent on data representation: its predictive MAE decreased from 3.22 to 0.84 °C and cvRMSE from 40.92% to 11.62% as coverage increased. A  similar trend was observed despite the total quantity of training data being held constant, demonstrating that broader operating-space representation was itself a critical factor in improving generalization.These findings demonstrate that model selection should not be based solely on the perceived or nominal advantages of a particular modeling approach. Rather, the characteristics of the available training data, particularly the extent to which they cover the intended operating domain, should be assessed prior to model development to determine which modeling approach is best suited to exploiting the available information.
KW  - training data coverage
KW  - pre-heating coil
KW  - outdoor air conditioning system
KW  - grey-box model
KW  - hybrid model
KW  - black-box model
KW  - model validity
KW  - replicative validity
KW  - predictive validity
KW  - operating domain
