TY  - JOUR
TI  - Enhancing the Usability of Dynamic Building Energy Simulation: Application to 149 Real-World Buildings
AU  - Lee, S. J.
AU  - Jo, H. G.
AU  - Park, C. H.
AU  - Park, C. S.
PY  - under review
AB  - Dynamic building energy simulation tools, such as EnergyPlus, can represent time-dependent thermal interactions in detail; however, their input requirements involve specialized knowledge and considerable time and effort for model development. These requirements have contributed to the perception that dynamic simulation is difficult to apply in practice. This study proposes and implements an approach to improve usability by simplifying user inputs while retaining EnergyPlus as the simulation engine. The input variables are designed based on building information available from drawings and documents, such as surface areas and azimuths, reducing the need to learn separate 3D modeling tools and acquire in-depth knowledge of EnergyPlus. Predefined rules, templates, and libraries translate these inputs into simulation-ready EnergyPlus models. To facilitate input entry and review, a spreadsheet-based interface presents the inputs in a familiar tabular format commonly used in design documents. The modeling procedure was first demonstrated using the U.S. DOE Medium Office reference building and subsequently applied to 149 public buildings in South Korea. Seven practitioners with no prior experience using EnergyPlus completed the models within two weeks following a two-hour hands-on workshop, requiring approximately half a day per building from document interpretation through simulation and result review. These findings suggest that the proposed approach can support the efficient application of dynamic simulation in building energy performance assessment.
KW  - dynamic building energy simulation
KW  - modeling efficiency
KW  - usability
KW  - simplified approach
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - From deterministic to stochastic performance gap: empirical quantification of stochasticity in 132 real-world buildings
AU  - Jo, H. G.
AU  - Lee, S. J.
AU  - Park, C. S.
PY  - under review
AB  - The measured–simulated energy discrepancy, commonly termed the building energy performance gap, is widely employed to assess model accuracy. However, measured energy use can vary even when building performance remains unchanged, making validation a moving-target problem. This study quantifies the corresponding moving range using four years (2018–2021) of electricity and gas records from 132 public buildings in South Korea, together with EnergyPlus simulations. This study separates the overall measured-simulated energy discrepancy into conditional mean discrepancy and realization deviation, assuming unchanged building specifications and operating regimes, along with zero-mean, normally distributed realization deviations of common variance. The moving range is defined as the interval expected to contain 99% of annual energy-use realizations, and its half-width is estimated by assuming an affine relationship between the conditional mean and simulated energy use. The estimated moving range half-width averaged 32.6 kWh/(m²·yr), with a normalized value of 16.5%. Under the adopted assumptions, annual energy use can therefore fluctuate around its conditional mean within nominal 99% bands averaging ±16.5% of mean measured energy use intensity (EUI). Analysis of selected buildings showed that apparent annual performance gaps can reflect realization variability even when conditional mean discrepancies are relatively small. The study proposes a stochastic performance gap (sPG) perspective in which validation accounts for both conditional mean agreement and realization variability, recognizing that individual energy discrepancies do not necessarily indicate correctable model deficiencies.
KW  - building energy performance gap
KW  - moving target
KW  - aleatory uncertainty
KW  - model validatoin
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Measured vs. Simulated post-retrofit energy-use in 149 public buildings using dynamic simulation
AU  - Lee, S. J.
AU  - Jo, H. G.
AU  - Yoo, Y. S.
AU  - Park, C. S.
PY  - 2026
JO  - Energy and Buildings, Vol. 372, 118260.
DO  - doi.org/10.1016/j.enbuild.2026.118260
AB  - Dynamic building energy simulation is widely used to examine “what-if” questions about how changes in building envelopes and systems affect energy use, particularly in retrofit evaluation. Yet measured energy-use changes in real buildings are shaped by both physical changes and uncertainties that are not fully represented in simulation models, making it unclear how simulation-based “what-if” results should be understood when measured–simulated discrepancies exist. Despite this limitation, the usefulness of simulation has predominantly been discussed in terms of its agreement with measured energy use at the individual-building level, while its explanatory value for what-if changes under real-world conditions has received less attention. In this study, this issue was evaluated by comparing measured data and EnergyPlus results for 149 retrofitted public non-residential buildings in South Korea, including daycare and healthcare buildings. Before- and after-retrofit EnergyPlus models were developed by integrating drawings, specifications, surveys, site-visit records, the Korean building energy code, and actual weather data. The simulation results were then compared with measured data in terms of energy use and before–after changes at the individual-building level, by energy conservation measure (ECM)-combination groups, and at the building-stock level. Results show that discrepancies between measured and simulated energy use were substantial at the individual-building level, whereas measured and simulated post-retrofit energy-use changes were similar at the building-stock level, both annually and monthly. This contrast was particularly evident for healthcare buildings, where the measured–simulated discrepancies in absolute annual energy use were 1,559.3 MWh/yr before retrofit and 1,346.0 MWh/yr after retrofit, whereas the discrepancy in annual energy savings was substantially smaller, at 213.3 MWh/yr. These findings suggest that before–after differencing and stock-level aggregation can help reduce the influence of building-specific deviations, allowing dynamic simulation to serve as a useful interpretive framework for understanding retrofit-induced energy-use changes in real buildings. Such an interpretation can extend the role of dynamic simulation in stock-level retrofit evaluation and decision-making under real-world uncertainty, particularly for policymakers and retrofit decision-makers, even when discrepancies remain for individual buildings.
KW  - bbuilding energy simulation
KW  - dynamic simulation
KW  - energy retrofit
KW  - energy use
KW  - performance gap
