AB  - Dynamic building energy simulation tools such as EnergyPlus can represent time-dependent thermal interactions in detail, but their complexinput requirements demand in-depth expertise and considerable time and effort. To address these it, this study proposes a simplifiedapproach to improve the usability of dynamic building energy simulation. The proposed approach uses a simplified set of input variableswhose values can be obtained from drawings and documents. A spreadsheet-based input interface allows users to review multiple inputstogether, while automated rules translate these inputs into simulation-ready EnergyPlus files. The method was applied to 149 public buildingsby practitioners without prior EnergyPlus experience, and the entire process from drawing interpretation to simulation and result reviewrequired approximately a half day per building. These results indicate the potential of the proposed approach for the efficient use ofdynamic simulation in building energy performance assessment
