TI  - Repeatability of Monthly Energy Use for 10,750 buildings
AU  - Ko, Y. D.
AU  - Lee, D. H.
AU  - Yoo, Y. S.
AU  - Jo, H. G.
AU  - Kim, D. W.
AU  - Park, C. S.
PY  - 2020
T2  - Proceedings of the Fall Conference of the Korean Institute of Architectural Sustainable Environment and Building Systems, pp. 145-146. Fall Conference of the Korean Institute of Architectural Sustainable Environment and Building Systems, 2020, November 19-21, 2020, Architecture Building, Korea National University of Transportation, Chungju, Republic of Korea; hybrid.
UR  - https://www.auric.or.kr/user/rdoc/DocRdoc.aspx?dn=400708&returnVal=RD_R
AB  - Although numerous studies have been conducted on performance gap between predicted and measured energy uses, there is a significant lack of evidence regarding whether monthly energy use of each building repeats over time. This study aims to evaluate the repeatability of monthly energy use of 10,750 buildings for three years (2017-2019). It is assumed that the higher CVRMSE of building’s monthly energy use between a pair of years is, the less repeatable a building is, leading to being less predictable. K-means algorithm was used to extract representative energy use patterns of the buildings, and four energy use patterns were identified. It is noteworthy that 9,760 (90.8%) buildings do not satisfy the monthly criteria of ASHRAE Guideline (CVRMSE≤0.15), which means their three years’ monthly energy use pattern do not repeat. This signifies that it might be difficult to predict the energy use of those buildings. In addition, it was found that there is a distinct difference of CVRMSE (i.e., the degree of unpredictability) among four energy
use patterns (0.27, 0.28 vs. 0.38, 0.40 on average), whereas there is little difference among seven building use types (0.27-0.31 on average).
KW  - building energy use
KW  - repeatability
KW  - energy use pattern
KW  - clustering
ER  -

